# Eclipses — a selection of real eclipses from 1980 to 2099 to visit, solar
# and lunar, in time order.
#
# Each is visited where it shows low in the sky, about 22 degrees up, so
# it is in view when looking toward the horizon, not overhead.
#
# A solar eclipse (the moon in front of the sun) has a moment (universal
# time) and a place on its central path (total or annular) where the sun
# stands about 22 degrees up; the eclipse is as total there as at its
# greatest point, only shorter. The dates and kinds are the published ones
# (NASA's Five Millennium Canon); the moments and places were found with
# lib/Moon itself, walking along each central path from the published
# greatest eclipse. The 2061 path runs only in the far north, where the sun
# stays near 11 degrees. The one partial eclipse is seen from 51N 53E, the
# sun 18 degrees up and 70% covered.
#
# A lunar eclipse (the moon in the earth's shadow, `lunar`) is seen from
# the whole night side of the earth at once. Its moment is its greatest
# eclipse as lib/Moon's lunarShadow finds it, within minutes of the
# published one, and its place one where the moon stands about 22 degrees
# up then, north of the point under the moon where that is at most 60
# degrees north, else turned to the west or east.
#
# Case 1258 checks that lib/Moon puts the moon over the sun at each solar
# one, and in the earth's umbra at each lunar one.
#
# The settings jump to one with eclipseClock: it turns the universal time
# into the local solar time the clock runs on (the longitude's hours), so
# the sky shows the eclipse from that place.
import Math
open Calendar

enum EclipseKind { total, annular, hybrid, partial }

type Eclipse {
  year: Int
  month: Int
  day: Int
  # the moment of greatest eclipse, hours of universal time
  utHours: Float
  kind: EclipseKind
  # the moon in the earth's shadow (a lunar eclipse), or the sun behind
  # the moon (a solar one)
  lunar: Bool = false
  # where to see it (degrees north, east): the sun or the moon about 22
  # degrees up
  latitude: Float
  longitude: Float
}

